\section{\appendix{}}

\section{lattice of projections}

Let $\mathcal{H}$ be a Hilbert space and $\mathcal{B(H)}$ the algebra of
bounded operators in $\mathcal{B(H)}$. By a projection in $\mathcal{B(H)}$ we
always mean an orthogonal projection. Recall that a projection $P$ in
$\mathcal{B(H)}$ is a bounded self-adjoint operator satisfying $P^{2}=P$. The
set of projections in $\mathcal{B(H)}$, although not forming a vector space,
has a very rich structure it is a a set with a partial ordering and is a
complete lattice. The lattice structure of the set of projections has profound
consequences on the structure of von Neumann algebras.

\subsection{1 The Lattice of Projections}

In Hilbert spaces there is a bijective correspondence between closed subspaces
and projections.This correspondence is given by%

\begin{equation}
P\leftrightarrow\operatorname*{Ran}(P),
\end{equation}


where $P$ is a projection and $\operatorname*{Ran}(P)$ denotes the range of
$P$.

Since the set of closed subspaces can be partially ordered by inclusion, we
can define a partial order $\leq$ in the set of projections using the above
correspondence:\qquad%
\begin{equation}
P\leq Q\Leftrightarrow\operatorname*{Ran}(P)\subseteq\operatorname*{Ran}(Q).
\end{equation}


But since projections are self-adjoint operators (in fact they are positive
operators, as $P=P^{\dagger}P$), they inherit the natural partial ordering of
self-adjoint operators, which we denote by $\leq_{sa}$, and whose definition
we recall now%

\begin{equation}
P\leq_{sa}Q\Leftrightarrow Q-P\geq0.
\end{equation}


As the following theorem shows, these two orderings coincide. Thus, we shall
not make any more distinctions of notation between them.

\begin{theorem}
Let $P,Q$ be projections in $\mathcal{B(H)}$. The following conditions are equivalent:

\begin{itemize}
\item
\begin{equation}
\operatorname*{Ran}(P)\subseteq\operatorname*{Ran}(Q),\,(\text{i.e. }P\leq Q)
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
QP=P
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
PQ=P
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert Px\right\Vert \leq\left\Vert Qx\right\Vert \forall x\in\mathcal{H}%
\end{equation}


\item
\begin{equation}
P\leq_{sa}Q
\end{equation}

\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}

\end{remark}

Two closed subspaces $Y,Z$ in $\mathcal{H}$ have

\begin{enumerate}
\item a \emph{greatest lower bound} $Y\wedge Z$ given by the intersection
$Y\cap Z$

and

\item a \emph{least upper bound} $Y\vee Z$ given by the closure of the
subspace generated by $Y$ and $Z$ i.e. $Y+Z$.
\end{enumerate}

Hence, if $P,Q$ are projections in $\mathcal{B(H)}$ then

\begin{enumerate}
\item $P\wedge Q$ is the projection onto $\operatorname*{Ran}(P)\cap
\operatorname*{Ran}(Q)$ and

\item $P\vee Q$ is the projection onto the closure of $\operatorname*{Ran}%
(P)+\operatorname*{Ran}(Q)$.
\end{enumerate}

The above discussion clarifies that the set of projections in $\mathcal{B(H)}$
has a lattice structure. In fact, the set of projections forms a complete
lattice, by above:

Every family $\{Y_{\U{3b1} }\}$ of closed subspaces in $\mathcal{H}$ possesses
an infimum $\U{22c0} Y_{\U{3b1} }$ and a supremum $\U{22c1} Y_{\U{3b1} }$,
which are, respectively, the intersection of all $Y_{\U{3b1} }$ and the
closure of the subspace generated by all $P_{\alpha}$. There is, of course, a
correspondent in terms of projections: every family $\{P_{\alpha}\}$ of
projections has an infimum $\U{22c0} P_{\alpha}$ and a supremum $\U{22c1}
P_{\alpha}$, which are, respectively, the projection onto the intersection of
all $\operatorname*{Ran}\left(  P_{\alpha}\right)  $ and the projection onto
the closure of the subspace generated by all $\operatorname*{Ran}\left(
P_{\alpha}\right)  .$

.

\subsection{2. Additional Lattice Features}

\begin{itemize}
\item The lattice of projections in $\mathcal{B(H)}$ is never distributive
(unless $\mathcal{H}$ is one-dimensional).

\item Also, it is modular $\operatorname*{Iff}$ $\mathcal{H}$ is finite
dimensional. Nevertheless, there are important in von Neumann algebras (a
particular type of subalgebras of $\mathcal{B(H)}$ that are \textquotedblright
rich\textquotedblright\ in projections) over an
infinite-dimensional$\mathcal{H}$, whose lattices of projections are in fact modular.

\item Projections on one-dimensional subspaces are usually called
\emph{minimal projections} and they are in fact minimal in the sense that:
there are no closed subspaces strictly between $\{0\}$ and a one-dimensional
subspace, and every closed subspace other than $\{0\}$ contains a
one-dimensional subspace. This means that the lattice of projections in
$\mathcal{B(H)}$ is an \emph{atomic lattice} and its atoms are precisely the
projections on one-dimensional subspaces. Moreover, every closed subspace of
$\mathcal{H}$ is the closure of the span of its one-dimensional subspaces.
Thus, the lattice of projections in $\mathcal{B(H)}$ is an atomic lattice.

\item In Hilbert spaces every closed subspace $Z$ is topologically
complemented by its orthogonal complement $(H=Z\oplus Z^{\bot})$, and this
fact is reflected in the structure of projections. The lattice of projections
is then an \emph{orthocomplemented lattice}, where the orthocomplement of each
projection $P$ is the projection $I-P$ (onto $\operatorname*{Ran}(P)$ ).

\item We shall see further ahead in this entry, when we discuss orthogonal
projections, that the lattice of projections in $\mathcal{B(H)}$ is an
\emph{orthomodular} \emph{lattice}.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{3. Commuting and Orthogonal Projections}

When two projections $P,Q$ commute, the projections $P\wedge Q$ and $P\vee Q$
can be described algebraically. We shall see at the end of this section that
$P$ and $Q$ commute precisely when its corresponding subspaces
$\operatorname*{Ran}(P)$ and $\operatorname*{Ran}(Q)$ are \textquotedblright
orthogonal\textquotedblright.

\begin{theorem}
Let $P,Q$ be commuting projections (i.e. $PQ=QP$), then
\end{theorem}

\begin{itemize}
\item $P\wedge Q=PQ$

\item $P\vee Q=P+Q-PQ$

\item $\operatorname*{Ran}(P)\vee\operatorname*{Ran}(Q)$=$\operatorname*{Ran}%
(P)+\operatorname*{Ran}(Q)$. In particular, $\operatorname*{Ran}%
(P)+\operatorname*{Ran}(Q)$ is closed.
\end{itemize}

Two projections $P,Q$ are said to be orthogonal if $P\leq Q^{\bot}$. This is
equivalent to say that its corresponding subspaces are orthogonal
$\operatorname*{Ran}(P)$ lies in the orthogonal complement of
$\operatorname*{Ran}(Q)$).

\begin{corollary}
1 - Two projections $P,Q$ are orthogonal $\operatorname*{Iff}$ $PQ=0$. When
this is so, then $P\vee Q=P+Q$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
2 - Let $P,Q$ be projections in $\mathcal{B(H)}$ such that $P\leq Q$. Then
$Q-P$ is the projection onto $\operatorname*{Ran}(Q)\cap\operatorname*{Ran}%
(P)$.
\end{corollary}

We can now see that $P,Q$ commute if and only if $\operatorname*{Ran}(Q)$ and
$\operatorname*{Ran}(P)$ are \textquotedblright
perpendicular\textquotedblright. A somewhat informal and intuitive definition
of \textquotedblright perpendicular\textquotedblright\ is that of requiring
the two subspaces to be orthogonal outside their intersection (this is
different to orthoganality , since orthogonal subspaces do not intersect each
other). More rigorously, $P$ and $Q$ commute if and only if the subspaces
$Ran(P)\cap(Ran(P)\cap Ran(Q))^{\bot}$ and $Ran(Q)\cap(Ran(P)\cap
Ran(Q))^{\bot}$ are orthogonal.

This can be proved using all the above results: The two subspaces are
orthogonal iff%
\begin{equation}
0=(P-P\wedge Q)(Q-P\wedge Q)=PQ-P\wedge Q\text{ and }PQ=P\wedge
Q\,\operatorname*{Iff}\,PQ=P\wedge Q=(P\wedge Q)^{\dagger}=(PQ)^{\dagger}=QP.
\end{equation}


We can now also see that the lattice of projections is orthomodular: Suppose
$P\leq Q$. Then, using the above results,%
\begin{equation}
P\vee(Q\wedge P^{\bot})=P\vee(Q-P)=P+(Q-P)-P(Q-P)=Q.
\end{equation}


\subsection{4 Nets of Projections}

In the following we discuss some useful and interesting results about
convergence and limits of projections. Let $\U{39b} $ be a poset. A net of
projections $\{P_{\U{3b1} }\},\U{3b1} \in\U{39b} $ is said to be increasing if
$\U{3b1} \leq\U{3b2} \Rightarrow P_{\U{3b1} }\leq P_{\U{3b2} }$. Decreasing
nets are defined similarly.

\begin{theorem}
3 - Let $\{P_{\U{3b1} }\}$ be an increasing net of projections. Then
$\lim\limits_{\U{3b1} }P_{\U{3b1} }x=\U{22c1} _{\U{3b1} }P_{\U{3b1} }x$ for
every $x\in\mathcal{H}$.
\end{theorem}

In other words, $P_{\U{3b1} }$ converges to $\U{22c1} _{\U{3b1} }P_{\U{3b1} }$
in the strong operator topology. Similarly for decreasing nets of projections,

\begin{theorem}
4 - Let $\{P_{\U{3b1} }\}$ be a decreasing net of projections. Then
$\lim\limits_{\U{3b1} }P_{\U{3b1} }x=\U{22c0} _{\U{3b1} }P_{\U{3b1} }x$ for
every $x\in\mathcal{H}$.
\end{theorem}

In other words, $P_{\U{3b1} }$ converges to $\U{22c0} _{\U{3b1} }P_{\U{3b1} }$
in the strong operator topology.

\begin{theorem}
5 - Let $\U{39b} $ be a set and $\{P_{\U{3b1} }\},\U{3b1} \in\U{39b} $ be a
family of pairwise orthogonal projections. Then $\U{2211} _{\alpha}$
$P_{\U{3b1} }$ is summable and $\U{2211} _{\alpha}P_{\U{3b1} }x=\U{22c1}
_{\U{3b1} }P_{\U{3b1} }x$ for all $x\in\mathcal{H}$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Symmetric bilinear forms}

Symmetric bilinear forms

The polarization identities are not restricted to inner products. If $B$ is
any symmetric bilinear form on a vector space, and $Q$ is the quadratic form
defined by
\begin{equation}
Q\left(  v\right)  =B\left(  v,v\right)
\end{equation}
then
\begin{equation}
2B\left(  u,v\right)  =Q\left(  u+v\right)  -Q\left(  u\right)  -Q\left(
v\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
2B\left(  u,v\right)  =Q\left(  u\right)  +Q\left(  v\right)  -Q\left(
u-v\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
4B\left(  u,v\right)  =Q\left(  u+v\right)  -Q\left(  u-v\right)
\end{equation}



